// P-PKEY S2: key-capable `@packed` structs member sets by CONTENT — literals, add/remove/
// contains, union/intersection, dedup, and display order (type name, then field-wise — the same
// total order packed map keys iterate in) all agree across both backends. `sorted()` on a list
// of key-capable packed structs orders the same way (one comparator).
// expect: stdout "2"
// expect: stdout "{Cell {x: -5, y: 0}, Cell {x: 1, y: 2}}"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: stdout "3"
// expect: stdout "{Cell {x: -5, y: 0}}"
// expect: stdout "{Cell {x: -5, y: 0}, Cell {x: 1, y: 2}, Cell {x: 9, y: 9}}"
// expect: stdout "[Cell {x: -5, y: 0}, Cell {x: 1, y: 2}, Cell {x: 7, y: 7}]"
// expect: stdout "{Pair {a: Cell {x: 1, y: 1}, flag: false}, Pair {a: Cell {x: 1, y: 1}, flag: true}}"
// expect: exit 0

// The ordering is DECLARED: `sorted()` hands the program an order, and what a `@packed`
// struct's field order means is the author's call, not the layout's.
@derive(Comparable)
@packed struct Cell { x: int; y: int }
@packed struct Pair { a: Cell; flag: bool }

// Literal + content dedup (the duplicate collapses).
mut s = #{Cell { x: 1, y: 2 }, Cell { x: -5, y: 0 }, Cell { x: 1, y: 2 }}
echo s.len()
echo s

echo s.contains(Cell { x: 1, y: 2 })
echo s.contains(Cell { x: 0, y: 0 })

s = s.add(Cell { x: 9, y: 9 })
s = s.add(Cell { x: 9, y: 9 })
echo s.len()

t = #{Cell { x: -5, y: 0 }, Cell { x: 42, y: 0 }}
echo s.intersection(t)
echo s.union(#{Cell { x: -5, y: 0 }})

// One comparator everywhere: sorted() over packed values uses the same content order.
echo [Cell { x: 7, y: 7 }, Cell { x: -5, y: 0 }, Cell { x: 1, y: 2 }].sorted()

// Nested + bool fields take part in identity and order (false < true).
echo #{Pair { a: Cell { x: 1, y: 1 }, flag: true }, Pair { a: Cell { x: 1, y: 1 }, flag: false }}
